Show several windows under the application's, each in a rectangle

With -u, one window of another client was shown under the application's
at a time, in all that the application's window leaves clear. Now the
application can have several shown at once, side by side: it gives each a
rectangle of its own window, and the window is put there.

The rectangle is given with the set_rectangle request of the
wlr-foreign-toplevel-management protocol, which is the one by which a
client says where in a surface of its own it represents a toplevel. From
the application, that is taken as where the window is to be: it is moved
there, told to be that size, and not drawn outside the rectangle, so that
a window that has not yet drawn itself at its new size takes nothing from
the one beside it. An empty rectangle takes the rectangle away, and the
window again has all that is left clear.

A window in a rectangle of its own is shown by the unset_minimized request,
beside whatever else is showing, and hidden by set_minimized. A window
without one is shown as before, in place of any other, and the activate
request still shows a window in place of any other, whichever it is.

The rectangle is taken to be one of the application's window whichever of
the application's surfaces the request names, and from any other client
the request is ignored. An application that speaks this protocol over a
connection of its own, beside its toolkit's, cannot name the surface its
toolkit draws on.

This is what lets the OpenGVA HMI show several programs' pictures at once
- the feeds of some of a vehicle's cameras - in tiles of one page.

A view's scene tree is also forgotten when the view is unmapped, where it
was left pointing at what had been destroyed: positioning a view looks at
it.

Upstream: not submitted

